<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/foundational/grammar-of-cinema">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Film is a language learned, not born into — and like language it has grammar. A
    <strong>shot</strong> is a word (wide = context, close = feeling, insert = information); a
    <strong>cut</strong> is syntax (what follows changes what the previous shot meant);
    <strong>montage</strong> builds thoughts no single image contains. The Kuleshov effect — the
    same expressionless face, shown before a bowl of soup or a coffin, reads as hunger or grief —
    is the founding demonstration that <em>meaning lives between the frames</em>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from a Paris café in 1895 to a fully digital cinema — the grammar evolved with the machinery" widget="timeline" config={filmTimeline} />

  <h2>The core grammar, in one scene</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Establish then compress:</strong> a wide shot tells you where everyone is; closer shots then trade geography for emotion.</li>
    <li><strong>The 180° rule</strong> keeps screen direction consistent so the audience never loses the map of who faces whom — breaks are allowed, but they should <em>mean</em> something.</li>
    <li><strong>Match on action</strong> hides the cut inside movement; jump cuts break it on purpose to make time visible.</li>
    <li><strong>Sound is half the film.</strong> Score tells you how to feel before anyone speaks; a room tone suddenly vanishing tells you more than dialogue could.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to watch like a professional</h2>
  <p>
    Rewatch one scene three times: once for story, once with sound only, once muted — counting
    cuts and noting where your eye goes. Every deliberate choice you notice is the director
    deciding you shouldn't notice it. That is the whole trick of the medium: invisible
    authorship.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bordwell & Thompson, Film Art: An Introduction; Lumière programme (1895); standard studio and festival histories.</p>
